WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:03.790
Noah: So, how are those tests?

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.163
Maya: Forty failing since this morning. I copy the errors into a chat, paste the answers into

00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:12.578
my editor, and the assistant doesn't even know my project.

00:00:12.828 --> 00:00:16.364
Noah: What if the agent lived right in your terminal?

00:00:16.614 --> 00:00:19.526
Maya: Another tool locked into a single model provider?

00:00:19.776 --> 00:00:21.766
Noah: Quite the opposite. Look.

00:00:22.516 --> 00:00:29.503
Seshat is an AI agent in the terminal. Open source, written in Go, shipped as a single

00:00:29.503 --> 00:00:30.072
binary.

00:00:30.322 --> 00:00:34.373
One command is enough on Linux, macOS or Windows.

00:00:34.373 --> 00:00:39.498
The download is checked with a checksum. Then you type seshat.

00:00:40.048 --> 00:00:41.463
Maya: And which model does it talk to?

00:00:41.613 --> 00:00:42.912
Noah: The one you choose.

00:00:43.562 --> 00:00:48.187
Ctrl-P opens the settings, then the providers.

00:00:48.187 --> 00:00:57.035
The built-in providers come first: Anthropic, OpenAI, Mistral, Gemini, DeepSeek, Ollama,

00:00:57.035 --> 00:00:59.348
OpenRouter, and others.

00:00:59.598 --> 00:01:03.547
Behind “more providers”, the open models.dev catalog:

00:01:03.547 --> 00:01:08.464
about one hundred and ninety providers, with no hand-written list.

00:01:08.464 --> 00:01:11.668
You search, you choose, you paste your key.

00:01:12.118 --> 00:01:13.773
Maya: And if I don't have an API key?

00:01:13.923 --> 00:01:17.702
Noah: With Codex, you sign in with your ChatGPT account:

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:22.313
a code, your browser, that's all. Copilot works the same way.

00:01:22.663 --> 00:01:27.308
To keep the code at home, Ollama runs a local model, no key needed.

00:01:27.558 --> 00:01:31.899
Ctrl-L lists only the models of the providers you have configured.

00:01:31.899 --> 00:01:35.056
You switch model in two keystrokes, mid-session.

00:01:35.306 --> 00:01:41.397
The main providers first. The rest of the catalog is open, and community feedback makes

00:01:41.397 --> 00:01:42.237
it reliable.

00:01:42.987 --> 00:01:46.497
Maya: OK. But does it really understand my project?

00:01:46.647 --> 00:01:47.435
Noah: Ask it.

00:01:47.885 --> 00:01:54.249
Shift-Tab turns on plan mode: the agent reads, searches and runs the tests, but changes

00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:57.395
nothing. Then it submits a plan for review.

00:01:57.645 --> 00:02:01.439
You can comment on it line by line before approving.

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:03.044
Only then does it act.

00:02:03.394 --> 00:02:07.718
It fixes, reruns the tests, and summarizes what it changed.

00:02:07.718 --> 00:02:10.649
This project is an illustrative example.

00:02:11.399 --> 00:02:13.802
Noah: And when the task is bigger, it delegates.

00:02:14.052 --> 00:02:19.415
One request, five sub-agents working in parallel, each with its own tools and context.

00:02:19.415 --> 00:02:24.841
This scene is replayed from a real session of the interface; the pace is reconstructed.

00:02:25.591 --> 00:02:27.377
Maya: And what if it runs a dangerous command?

00:02:27.527 --> 00:02:29.434
Noah: It asks before it acts.

00:02:29.784 --> 00:02:37.877
Allow, allow for the session, or deny: you see the command or the change before it goes

00:02:37.877 --> 00:02:45.133
out. Six permission modes, from “ask first” to “never ask” for automated runs.

00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:49.283
Secrets are not passed to the commands the model runs.

00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:53.978
A cloned project's configuration is ignored until you approve it.

00:02:53.978 --> 00:02:58.312
And on Linux, a sandbox limits writes to the working folder.

00:02:59.062 --> 00:03:05.614
In the prompt, an exclamation mark runs a command in a side panel; two, and the agent

00:03:05.614 --> 00:03:12.550
reads the output. Slash calls your skills, at attaches a file, backslash opens the command

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:13.167
palette.

00:03:13.417 --> 00:03:23.438
Resume a session with --continue, run a task in the background with seshat run --bg,

00:03:23.438 --> 00:03:27.732
and seshat doctor checks your setup.

00:03:28.482 --> 00:03:29.832
Maya: And all of this stays mine?

00:03:29.982 --> 00:03:36.589
Noah: The runtime is open source, Apache 2.0. Your sessions stay on your machine, and memory

00:03:36.589 --> 00:03:37.972
is off by default.

00:03:38.322 --> 00:03:46.908
MCP to plug in tools, skills for the team's rules, a Go SDK to embed it.

00:03:46.908 --> 00:03:52.632
The same engine powers SeshatOS and SeshatCloud.

00:03:54.182 --> 00:03:55.904
Maya: Well. My tests?

00:03:56.054 --> 00:03:58.764
Noah: Green. And you approved every change.

00:03:59.514 --> 00:04:05.824
Seshat. One runtime. Any model. Any language.
